7592186278095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278095 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186277492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278095 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278095 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278095 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186275629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278095 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d3963dbe-4605-5d7a-9bdb-2239345ae704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161311" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278475 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5b/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc8526c3-3b66-50ba-a72f-6803fda09a5e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278475 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186278095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161339" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278486 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d19d54d0e76ced24d1bd9f865578b72f2b8d67414c8506499c6ae7b1300a6275.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-2x+2y$ and whose domain is the left half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e935e93c-c375-5e87-b42b-951f2a795ef1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278486 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-161341" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278488 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b54f01b4-5da4-54d3-8529-0362ccff055b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186278488 #edb/ref 1074 "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}4z\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,4(x+y)\\,\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4(x+y)\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[4xy+2{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{[2{x}^{2}+2x]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415951 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{121}{3}\\sqrt{26}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415951 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415951 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "20eaebd4-47b4-5a06-b816-cc531888309e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759